     "key": "EXACT-SAMPLE-SHRINKING-BIN-LEMMA",
     "kind": "theorem-proof",
     "statement": "For the smooth periodized Gaussian field on the L=24 torus with exact vertex samples and the vertex cubical superlevel filtration, if h^2 sqrt(log(1/h)) = o(tau_h) then E|N_h(I_h)-N(I_h)|/E N(I_h) -> 0 for shrinking lifetime bins I_h=[lambda tau_h, mu tau_h), so E N_h(I_h)/E N(I_h) -> 1; proved via a deterministic sharp-bin boundary-strip lemma under an eps-matching, the cubical displacement bound d_h <= h^2 H(f)/2, M4 boundary mass, and a Borell-TIS / marked Kac-Rice bad-event bound E[N_crit 1{H>T}] <= C exp(-cT^2).",
     "status_in_post": "author-side analytic lemma, claim released; asymptotic exact-sample statement only (no FFT/roundoff/finite-grid certificate). The repo reading record later records nonauthor PASS_TECHNICAL_SCOPED (Codex 5976527971) and a Claude scoped review (5976979978)."
    },
    {
     "key": "main-229/5976527971/shrinking-bin-explicit-threshold",
     "kind": "review-verdict-with-new-math",
     "statement": "Nonauthor PASS_TECHNICAL_SCOPED review of the exact-sample shrinking-bin lemma (main#229/5976299711). It adds two small pieces of mathematics.\n(a) An explicit sufficient constant replaces the lemma's 'choose fixed A large enough'. Let c be the smaller tail exponent of (5)-(6), and use tau_h >= h^2 sqrt(log(1/h)), which holds eventually. Then cA^2 > 10/3 suffices, because the bad-event ratio is at most C h^(cA^2-10/3) log(1/h)^(-1/3) = o(1).\n(b) An alternate route to (6): Fourier Theorem F (Math- c6_fourier_cutoff PROOF.md, blob 1d9177a2) gives E N_crit^2 < infinity for the unconditioned total ",
     "status_in_post": "Borderline accept. The verdict is PASS_TECHNICAL_SCOPED with no required amendment, and both additions are minor and non-blocking. The later Anthropic review 5976979978 agrees with cA^2>10/3 and suggests recording it (N1). Organizational-independence credit 0. No Lean check or status change."
    },
